A||N||3||Monkeyman||Painting the MC||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||08-05-2000||01:34 PM||||Is there any reason why I can't paint my new MC?  I just bought some purple paint (for my new calipers--too dark, but it'll have to do) and I thought I might as well color coordinate some other stuff while I'm doing my calipers.<P>Also,  I just bought the parts to do the rear brakes (might as well do the whole thing at once and bleed the brakes once).  The pads I got are painted red.  Is there any way to remove the paint without damaging the pads?  I hadn't planned on painting them purple, but I don't want red pads with purple calipers, etc.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||08-05-2000||03:52 PM||||By MC do you mean master cylinder? If so, I suppose you could paint it, however, keep in mind that brake fluid to paint is like, uh, brake fluid to paint!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Brake fluid eats through paint rather quickly, and by painting something such as the plastic resourvour (how in the hell do yo spell that?) it wouldnt take anytime for the paint to come off.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||08-05-2000||09:05 PM||||Red pads/w purple calipers would look good. If you still want to remove the paint, use brake cleaner on the pads. It sure did a number on my valve covers. You can paint the MC if you want to. Just clean any spilled  brake fluid off ASAP. They make chemical resistant paints that people use too. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||08-06-2000||01:56 AM||||MC=master cylinder.  I hadn't given much thought to the paint removing qualities of brake fluid.  I guess I'll just leave the MC alone.  Maybe when I get around to doing some custom work to the front compartment (way in the future if at all), I'll think about it.  I didn't really want to buy any other special paint.  <P>The more I think about it, the more I have to agree, Pontiaddict.  Red pads/purple calipers.  Hmmm...  What the heck.  I'll just leave them alone for now.  I can always remove them and strip and paint them later.  Now I just need to get some hi-temp <B>red</B> paint.  The pads from the Fiero Store (front kit) are generic black.  I think I'll derust (is that a word?) my front sway bar and paint that purple, too.  I have to replace the end links anyway.  <P>Anyone know of a good source for the bushings that hold the middle of the sway bar in place?  I got the (poly) end links from the Fiero Store, but they didn't seem to carry the middle parts.  Autozone only carries them in rubber.  I need to get some soon as I plan to do all of this in the next week or so.  By the end of the month at the latest.  I'm almost getting excited.||||reg||1
